! The interruption mechanism: fire, run ONE instruction, and return
! with the instruction counter where it was.
!
! THE RULES, all Reference Manual p.48.
!
! "Thus, interruption requires the mask bit to be 1, the indicator bit
! to be 1, and the mechanism to be enabled."  This program supplies all
! three and removes them one at a time in the injections.
!
! "When a program interruption occurs, a mechanism develops the number
! of the leftmost actuated indicator whose mask bit is one.  After this
! leftmost indicator has been turned off, the developed number is added
! to the contents of the interruption address register to form the
! effective interruption address.  This address does not replace the
! contents of the instruction counter but is used to fetch a single
! instruction which is executed before a second interruption can
! occur."
!
! "The instruction counter is stepped neither before nor during the
! execution of the instruction at the effective interruption address;
! however, the instruction counter can be replaced as the result of a
! successful branching operation."
!
! WHY PG0.  p.46: "41-47.  Program Indicators Zero through Six
! (PG0-PG6).  These indicators are set by programming only."  So the
! provocation needs no arithmetic fault and no I/O -- the program turns
! the indicator on itself, deterministically, and 41 is inside the
! settable mask range.  p.48 sanctions both writes: "Individual mask
! bits with addresses 12.20 through 12.47 can be set or changed by
! connective operations, arithmetic operations, or BRANCH ON BIT", and
! BRANCH ON BIT reaches indicator bits 20-63 (p.41).  Both of those
! paths are already transcribed and tested here (bb_indicator.strap,
! the mask's fixed/writable fields).
!
! THE DECK DOES EXACTLY THIS, and it was found after this program was
! designed, not before: jb_ax5b routine 1260 (PDF p.45) sets one
! indicator bit in word 11, sets "MASK BIT OF TEST IND BIT" in word 12,
! executes "BE,$+.32  -ENABLE AND AWAIT INTERRUPT", and falls through
! to an error path commented "FAILED TO INTERRUPT ON IND MK".  That
! block is not transcribed yet -- it needs SSIP's save/restore harness
! -- so this program remains the WEAKER STANDARD, hand-written from
! p.48.  See docs/NEXT.md for what transcribing 1260 would settle.
!
! THE UNITS ARE WORDS, AND THIS PROGRAM PINS THAT.  p.48: "The initial
! instruction for a routine occupies all or half of a location in a
! table of 48 successive full-word locations.  The address of the first
! location is contained in the interruption address register."  So the
! developed number counts WORDS.  If it counted half-words instead, an
! interruption on indicator 41 would land at half-word TBL*2+41 rather
! than word TBL+41 -- forty-one half-words away -- and HWRONG below
! sits exactly there to catch it.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD: hand-written from p.48.

        SLC, (8)4100.0
!
! ---- set up, with the system disabled ------------------------------
! Word 2 is permanently protected (p.31), so the interruption address
! can only be written while disabled -- which is what SSIP's SIP16 does
! before it rewrites words 1, 2 and 3.
START   BD, $+.32                    ! disable
        LVI, $X13, TBL               ! the table origin, as a bit address
        SV,  $X13, $IA               ! interruption address := TBL (word 2, 0-17)
        BB1, 12.41, $+1.0            ! mask bit 41 on: PG0 may interrupt
        BB,  MARK, $                 ! hang if MARK is already set -- it must not be
        BB,  $PG0, $                 ! hang if PG0 is already on
!
! ---- two of the three conditions: NOTHING may happen ----------------
! Mask bit 1 and indicator bit 1, but the mechanism disabled.  p.48
! requires all three, so this must be inert -- and it is the half of
! the test that a model ignoring the enable bit would fail.
        BB1, $PG0, $+1.0             ! turn PG0 on while DISABLED
        BZB, $PG0, $                 ! hang if PG0 went off by itself
        BB,  MARK, $                 ! hang if MARK is set: nothing may have run
!
! ---- the third condition: fire ---------------------------------------
! BE acts unconditionally and before the branch (p.40), so the
! interruption follows this instruction, not some later one.
        BE,  $+.32                   ! enable
!
! Execution resumes HERE, because the routine at TBL+41 does not
! branch: "the instruction counter is stepped neither before nor
! during" it.
AFTER   BZB, MARK, $                 ! hang if MARK is off: the routine never ran
        BB,  $PG0, $                 ! hang if PG0 is still on: it must be turned
                                     !   off BEFORE the address is formed
!
! ---- the mask is REQUIRED, and only the masked indicator is chosen --
! THIS BLOCK EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIRST ONE COULD NOT SEE THE MASK.  An
! injection replacing "indicators & mask" with "indicators" passed
! everything above: the only indicator the first block turns on is the
! one it also masks, so honouring the mask and ignoring it give the
! same answer.  That is the "a green test is only evidence about faults
! its inputs could have made visible" rule catching this file, and the
! fix is an indicator that is ON and NOT masked.
!
! PG0 (41) is left unmasked and on; PG2 (43) is masked and on.  p.48
! requires all three conditions per indicator, so the interruption must
! be taken on 43 and 41 must be left completely alone -- neither
! interrupting nor, since only "this leftmost indicator has been turned
! off", cleared.  A model ignoring the mask would take 41 first,
! because 41 is further left, and clear it.
        BD,  $+.32                   ! disable to rearrange the mask
        BBZ, 12.41, $+1.0            ! mask bit 41 OFF -- PG0 may not interrupt
        BB1, 12.43, $+1.0            ! mask bit 43 ON  -- PG2 may
        BB1, $PG0, $+1.0             ! PG0 on: actuated but NOT masked
        BB1, $PG2, $+1.0             ! PG2 on: actuated AND masked
        BB,  MARK2, $                ! hang if MARK2 is already set
        BE,  $+.32                   ! enable
AFTER2  BZB, MARK2, $                ! hang if MARK2 is off: 43's routine never ran
        BB,  $PG2, $                 ! hang if PG2 is still on
        BZB, $PG0, $                 ! hang if PG0 went OFF -- unmasked, it must be
                                     !   neither taken nor turned off
!
! ---- the counter IS replaced by a successful branch ----------------
! p.48: "The instruction counter is stepped neither before nor during
! the execution of the instruction at the effective interruption
! address; however, the instruction counter can be replaced as the
! result of a SUCCESSFUL BRANCHING OPERATION."  Both blocks above use a
! routine that falls through, so neither can see that clause -- which
! is the same blindness the mask block was added to fix, and it is
! listed in NEXT.md rather than left implied.
!
! PG3's routine is a branch.  NORET is where the counter would come
! back to if the branch were ignored, and it hangs; RESUME is where the
! branch goes.  p.48-49's correction-routine pattern is exactly this
! shape -- "a subroutine entered by STORE-INSTRUCTION-COUNTER-IF-
! BRANCH-DISABLED and returned by BRANCH ENABLED".
!
! PG0 is still actuated and still unmasked here, so the masked PG3 must
! be chosen over it a second time, with a different routine.
        BD,  $+.32                   ! disable to set the mask
        BB1, 12.44, $+1.0            ! mask bit 44 on -- PG3 may interrupt
        BB1, $PG3, $+1.0             ! PG3 on
        BE,  $+.32                   ! enable: the interruption follows
NORET   B, $                         ! HANG -- the counter must NOT return here
RESUME  BB,  $PG3, $                 ! hang if PG3 is still on
!
! ---- LEFTMOST wins, and only IT is turned off ----------------------
! p.48: "a mechanism develops the number of the LEFTMOST actuated
! indicator whose mask bit is one", and the programming note says why
! it matters: "the leftmost one identification defines the relative
! priority of actuated indicators if more than one has its
! corresponding mask bit turned on when interruption occurs.  If the
! interruption instruction does not disable the system in such a case,
! then another succeeding interruption will be caused by the new
! leftmost selected indicator BEFORE the execution of an instruction
! addressed by the instruction counter."
!
! Every block above actuates exactly ONE masked indicator, so none of
! them can tell leftmost from rightmost -- a model picking the
! rightmost masked indicator passes all of them.  This one actuates
! two, PG4 (45) and PG5 (46), and PG0 (41) is STILL on and still
! unmasked from the mask block, so three different wrong rules give
! three different answers: ignore the mask and 41 is chosen, take the
! rightmost and 46 is, take the leftmost MASKED and 45 is.
!
! HOW THE ORDER IS OBSERVED, given that both routines run.  PG4's
! routine SETS a bit in ORDER and falls through.  PG5's routine BRANCHES
! on that same bit: it can only find it already set if PG4 went first.
! So the branch is taken exactly when the order was right, and the
! branch is also what moves the counter off WRONG -- which hangs.
!
! It witnesses three rules at once: leftmost rather than rightmost;
! only THAT indicator turned off, since PG5 must still be actuated to
! cause the second interruption; and the second interruption arriving
! before the counter's own next instruction runs.
        BD,  $+.32                   ! disable to set the mask
        BB1, 12.45, $+1.0            ! mask bit 45 on -- PG4
        BB1, 12.46, $+1.0            ! mask bit 46 on -- PG5
        BB1, $PG4, $+1.0             ! PG4 on
        BB1, $PG5, $+1.0             ! PG5 on
        BB,  ORDER, $                ! hang if ORDER is already set
        BE,  $+.32                   ! enable: two interruptions follow
WRONG   B, $                         ! HANG -- reached if PG5's routine did
                                     !   not find PG4's mark, i.e. the wrong
                                     !   indicator was taken first
GOOD    BB,  $PG4, $                 ! hang if PG4 is still on
        BB,  $PG5, $                 ! hang if PG5 is still on
        B, PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the interruption table ----------------------------------------
! Only the three words around indicator 41 are populated; nothing else
! is ever fetched if the mechanism is right, and a landing outside them
! runs into untouched storage, which halts the run at the step cap.
        SLC, (8)4450.0
E40     B, $                         ! indicator 40 -- must not be reached
        SLC, (8)4451.0
E41     BB1, MARK, $+1.0             ! PG0's routine: ONE instruction, and it
                                     !   does NOT branch, so the counter stands
        SLC, (8)4452.0
E42     B, $                         ! indicator 42 -- must not be reached
        SLC, (8)4453.0
E43     BB1, MARK2, $+1.0            ! PG2's routine, for the mask block below
        SLC, (8)4454.0
E44     B, RESUME                    ! PG3's routine: a BRANCH, so it replaces
                                     !   the instruction counter
        SLC, (8)4455.0
E45     BB1, ORDER, $+1.0            ! PG4 (leftmost): leave a mark, fall through
        SLC, (8)4456.0
E46     BB,  ORDER, GOOD             ! PG5 (second): branch only if PG4's mark is
                                     !   already there -- i.e. only if the order
                                     !   was right
!
! Where HALF-word indexing would land instead of word indexing:
! TBL*2 + 41 half-words = word 04424, second half.  Reached only if the
! developed number is added in the wrong units.
!
! NOTE THE .40.  A "(8)" radix prefix applies to BOTH halves of a bit
! address, so the offset here is OCTAL too: .40 is 32 bits, the second
! half-word.  Written ".32" first, which is octal 32 = 26 bits, and the
! assembler refused it -- "alignment gap at bit 148762 is 6 bits, not a
! whole number of half-words".  The same decimal/octal confusion that
! put seven wrong addresses in run_tests.sh last session; caught here
! by the assembler instead of by review.
        SLC, (8)4424.40
HWRONG  B, $
!
! ---- data ----------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)4300.0
MARK    DD, 0                        ! the routine's only side effect
        SLC, (8)4301.0
MARK2   DD, 0                        ! the same, for the mask block
        SLC, (8)4302.0
ORDER   DD, 0                        ! PG4's mark, read by PG5's routine
        SLC, (8)4400.0
TBL     DD, 0                        ! the table origin; entry N is TBL+N words
        END, START
